  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-8-2010-p16.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let \\(s\\) be the side length of the square and let \\(r\\) be the radius of the circle. Then, since they have the same areas, \\(s^2 = \\pi r^2 .\\) This means \\((\\dfrac sr)^2 = \\pi,\\) so \\(\\frac sr = \\sqrt{ \\pi} .\\)\n\nThus, the answer is B .",
      "hints": [
        "Set \\(s^2=\\pi r^2\\) and solve for \\(\\frac{s}{r}\\).",
        "Write the square area and circle area in terms of side length and radius."
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
